body:
This seems to have fixed the issue (I think*):

```diff
diff --git a/src/nvim/eval.c b/src/nvim/eval.c
index b5a2ff93..8aa420ca 100644
--- a/src/nvim/eval.c
+++ b/src/nvim/eval.c
@@ -6060,7 +6060,8 @@ static int free_unref_items(int copyID)
   // PASS 2: free the items themselves.
   for (dd = first_dict; dd != NULL; dd = dd_next) {
     dd_next = dd->dv_used_next;
-    if ((dd->dv_copyID & COPYID_MASK) != (copyID & COPYID_MASK)) {
+    if ((dd->dv_copyID & COPYID_MASK) != (copyID & COPYID_MASK)
+        && dd->dv_refcount == 0) {
       dict_free_dict(dd);
     }
   }
```

This was a shot in the dark and I definitely don't know enough about how the garbage collection works to say that this is an actual fix.  This is most likely just preventing the reaping of dicts.  1cf7de074 changed this line but `dv_refcount` wasn't involved.

\* The reason I say "I think" above is that I'm not entirely sure if another crash occurred.  While I was running the debug build (ASAN enabled), I was testing by clicking in and out of the terminal a couple dozen times.  Since I'm doing this with vimfiler open, I *might* have accidentally double clicked and opened a backtrace log file, mistaking it as nvim crashing.  The core dump I found said that it was generated by another binary.  So, now I'm full of doubt 😰

Apparently dict functions just don't work in e97e24c.  I spent a little time going back and forth between nvim and vim's sources to look for obvious differences and did a little trial and error debugging.  The only thing that keeps me from reproducing the crash is using Vim's version of `clear_tv()`

```diff
diff --git a/src/nvim/eval.c b/src/nvim/eval.c
index 6b14d21d..20e575ef 100644
--- a/src/nvim/eval.c
+++ b/src/nvim/eval.c
@@ -19322,10 +19322,38 @@ static inline void _nothing_conv_dict_end(typval_T *const tv,
 /// @param[in,out]  varp  Value to free.
 void clear_tv(typval_T *varp)
 {
-  if (varp != NULL && varp->v_type != VAR_UNKNOWN) {
-    const int evn_ret = encode_vim_to_nothing(varp, varp, "clear_tv argument");
-    (void)evn_ret;
-    assert(evn_ret == OK);
+  if (varp != NULL) {
+    switch (varp->v_type) {
+      case VAR_FUNC:
+        func_unref(varp->vval.v_string);
+        // FALLTHROUGH
+      case VAR_STRING:
+        xfree(varp->vval.v_string);
+        varp->vval.v_string = NULL;
+        break;
+      case VAR_PARTIAL:
+        partial_unref(varp->vval.v_partial);
+        varp->vval.v_partial = NULL;
+        break;
+      case VAR_LIST:
+        list_unref(varp->vval.v_list);
+        varp->vval.v_list = NULL;
+        break;
+      case VAR_DICT:
+        dict_unref(varp->vval.v_dict);
+        varp->vval.v_dict = NULL;
+        break;
+      case VAR_NUMBER:
+      case VAR_SPECIAL:
+        varp->vval.v_number = 0;
+        break;
+      case VAR_FLOAT:
+        varp->vval.v_float = 0.0;
+        break;
+      case VAR_UNKNOWN:
+        break;
+    }
+    varp->v_lock = 0;
   }
 }
```

I'm pretty sure this is still leaky, though.  If something in `encode_vim_to_nothing()` is causing the crash, I can't figure it out because it's a little too magical for me to grok.

---

Something weird also happened while I was messing with this.  After some time, reproduction of the crash became inconsistent (with no changes to the source).  I even ran `distclean` and rebuilt a couple of times.  When I started, I was able to reproduce the crash without fail.
